# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Template for the environment file.
#
# This file IS committed and holds no secrets. Copy it to .env if you want
# one — .env is git-ignored, so anything you put there stays on your machine.
#
#   Windows (PowerShell):  Copy-Item .env.example .env
#   macOS / Linux:         cp .env.example .env
#
# You do NOT need this file to run the app. There is nothing to configure
# except the port, and the port has a working default.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why there is no API key here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The chat lab's .env.example starts with ANTHROPIC_API_KEY. This one does
# not, and that absence is the point: THIS SERVER NEVER CALLS ANTHROPIC.
#
# An MCP server does not talk to a model. It exposes tools, resources and
# prompts, and waits for a client to call them. The model sits on the far
# side of that client — in the chat lab, or in Claude Desktop — and this
# process never sees it, never sends it anything, and never needs a key to
# do its job.
#
# That is worth noticing rather than skipping past. "Where does the key
# live?" is the question that tells you which component talks to the model,
# and in an MCP setup the answer is never the MCP server.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Port the inspector and the MCP HTTP endpoint listen on.
#
# 3100 rather than 3000 so this can run at the same time as the chat lab,
# which is the configuration the two projects are designed for: the chat
# lab on 3000 connecting to the MCP server on 3100, with both activity
# logs open side by side.
PORT=3100
